Yes — it can be done with GitHub Copilot + Claude Opus 4.5, especially if you have the repository in GitHub and Copilot agent/cloud agent is enabled. GitHub currently supports Claude Opus 4.5 as a Copilot coding-agent model, and the agent can research a repository, create an implementation plan, make changes on a branch, run/modify tests, and iterate based on your feedback.

But I would not ask Copilot to "convert this monolith into microservices" in one shot. That's where you risk getting a technically valid but architecturally poor result.

For the architecture you're describing:

                 Experience API
                       |
          +------------+------------+
          |            |            |
     Capability 1  Capability 2  Capability 3
          |            |            |
       Domain       Domain       Domain
       Service      Service      Service
          |            |            |
      Integration  Integration  Integration
          |            |            |
      PolicyCenter / Earnix / other systems

I'd use Copilot as an implementation engineer, while you remain the architect.

The approach I'd recommend

Phase 1 — Have Copilot understand the existing monolith

Ask it to analyze, but make no code changes:

Analyze this repository as a senior application architect. Identify the current Experience API structure, business capabilities, domain responsibilities, external system integrations, shared components, dependencies, and cross-cutting concerns. Do not modify any files. Produce an architecture assessment and identify potential boundaries for Experience, Capability, Domain Service, and Integration layers.

Copilot's cloud agent specifically supports repository research and planning before implementation.



Phase 2 — You define the target architecture

This is the part I would not delegate completely to AI.

For example, you decide:

Experience API
      |
      +---- Quote Capability API
      |          |
      |          +---- Quote Domain Service
      |                     |
      |                     +---- PolicyCenter Adapter
      |
      +---- Driver Capability API
                 |
                 +---- Driver Domain Service
                            |
                            +---- PolicyCenter Adapter

You also decide things such as:

What constitutes a Capability
What belongs in Domain Services
Where business rules live
What belongs in Integration
Command vs Query separation
Which APIs should be synchronous
Where caching belongs
Circuit breaker / timeout / retry boundaries
Whether a capability gets its own deployable service
Data ownership
API contracts

Those are architecture decisions, not merely coding decisions.

Phase 3 — Give Copilot your architecture rules

This is where Copilot becomes extremely useful.

You can create something like:

.github/
   copilot-instructions.md

and document your architectural rules.

GitHub supports repository-level Copilot instructions specifically for giving Copilot persistent knowledge about project structure, conventions, build/test procedures, etc.

For your project, I'd put rules such as:

Architecture Principles


1. Experience APIs must not directly call Guidewire or other external systems.


2. Capability APIs own business capabilities and must not contain
   external-system-specific integration logic.


3. Domain Services contain business logic and orchestration.


4. Integration Services are responsible for communication with
   external systems such as Guidewire PolicyCenter and Earnix.


5. No domain service may directly depend on a Guidewire SDK/client.


6. External system calls must implement timeout, retry and circuit breaker
   according to the resiliency standards.


7. Controllers must not contain business logic.


8. DTOs used by external systems must not leak into the domain model.


9. Existing behavior must be preserved unless explicitly identified
   as a business-rule change.


10. Every refactoring step must have automated tests.

That dramatically changes the quality of what Copilot produces.

Phase 4 — Refactor one capability at a time

For example, don't say:

Refactor the entire Experience API.

Instead:

Extract Vehicle Management capability from the existing Experience API.

Then give Copilot very specific instructions:

Identify all code related to Vehicle Management.


Create an implementation plan that shows:
1. Existing classes involved
2. Existing dependencies
3. Business logic currently embedded in Experience API
4. Guidewire dependencies
5. Proposed Capability API
6. Proposed Domain Service
7. Proposed Integration Adapter
8. Classes that should be moved
9. Classes that should remain
10. Tests that need to be created or modified


Do not modify the code yet.

Review the plan.

Then:

Implement the approved plan. Preserve existing API behavior and
functionality. Do not introduce new business behavior. Create tests
for the extracted components.

This incremental approach is much safer.

One important limitation

If your target architecture requires creating multiple repositories, Copilot cloud agent has a significant limitation: a cloud-agent task operates on the repository specified when you start it and cannot make changes across multiple repositories in one run.

So if your current monolith is one repository and you're ultimately going to create:

experience-api
vehicle-capability-api
vehicle-domain-service
policycenter-integration
earnix-integration

you'll probably want to do the refactoring in stages.

You could initially create the architectural separation within the existing repository, then split services/repositories afterward.

My recommendation for your situation

Given that you're acting as the Solution Architect, I would use:

You → Architecture & boundaries

Claude Opus 4.5/Copilot → Repository analysis + implementation

Copilot → Tests + refactoring + dependency analysis

You → Review every architectural decision

The combination is absolutely capable of helping you do this. In fact, Copilot's agent workflow is specifically designed around research → plan → implement → review → iterate, rather than simply generating snippets.

And you don't necessarily need Claude's separate subscription for this—the Claude coding agent can be powered by an existing Copilot subscription when available to your plan.

If you want, I can help you build the exact .github/copilot-instructions.md and a sequence of 10–15 Copilot prompts for your specific Experience → Capability → Domain → Integration transformation. That would be much more useful than giving Copilot one giant refactoring prompt.